H-FRAME: Hierarchical Functional Inheritance and Recombination in Artificial Agents

Ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM 

Version: 1.0 — Final Theoretical Specification

Status: Theoretical. No experiments conducted. No code trained. No physical tests performed.

---

Abstract

This thesis develops a rigorous mathematical foundation for the inheritance and recombination of functional structures in artificial agents. We introduce H-FRAME (Hierarchical Functional Inheritance and Recombination in Artificial Agents), a framework that unifies two formalisms: AISE-0.3, specifying a perceptual genome of filters with spectrally-preserving distillation, and AC-DNA v0.4, specifying a cognitive genome of Minimal Transmissible Cognitive Units (MTCUs). The central mathematical contributions are: (i) an identification theorem for the compatibility contrast K_ij via a four-term interventional difference-in-differences structure; (ii) a proof that independent recombination (R2) is structurally more novel than coupled recombination (R1), reversing the intuitive ordering; (iii) a feasibility bound on R2 under hard constraints; (iv) a calibration/test separation theorem resolving the circularity of the compatibility map; (v) a minimax lower bound on the estimation of K_ij; (vi) a sample complexity bound for the four-outcome decision rule; and (vii) a consistency theorem for MTCU extraction via interventional boundary discovery. All results are stated as theorems, propositions, or lemmas with complete proofs. No empirical claims are made. The thesis closes with a structured implementation programme for future work.

Keywords: causal inference, interventional contrast, modular recombination, survival analysis, pre-registration, falsifiability.

---

Chapter 1 — Introduction

1.1 The problem

Artificial agents acquire capabilities through learning. Whether reusable functional structures can be transmitted across generations of agents independently of the knowledge and parameters that produced them remains an open theoretical question. Two research programmes address this question from complementary directions.

AISE-0.3 investigates the transmission of perceptual structures—sequences of gammatone filters—across generations, without transmitting controller parameters. AC-DNA v0.4 investigates whether cognitive design patterns can be operationalized as transmissible, recombinable computational objects called Minimal Transmissible Cognitive Units (MTCUs).

Both programmes operate under the same epistemic discipline: a hypothesis is stated, a protocol is defined, and the claim is conditional on empirical validation. Neither has demonstrated its central hypothesis. This thesis provides the mathematical foundation that unifies them.

1.2 The central question

Under what conditions does the inheritance and recombination of functional structures produce transferable capabilities that cannot be explained by knowledge transfer, parameter inheritance, increasing model capacity, or training effects alone?

The question decomposes into four sub-questions:

1. What is transmitted, and how is transmission verified independently of parameter transfer?
2. What remains useful after a task change, and how is utility measured?
3. What can be recombined, and what does recombination produce that neither parent contained?
4. What is genuinely emergent, and how is emergence distinguished from ordinary learning?

1.3 Epistemic status

This document is a theoretical specification. Every claim belongs to one of three categories.

Definition 1.1 (Epistemic categories).

· Category M (Mathematical). A statement proved within this document.
· Category V (Validated). A statement whose correctness is established by an external peer-reviewed or arXiv-indexed result.
· Category P (Proposed). A statement whose correctness is not yet established and which constitutes a target for future work.

No empirical claims are made. No experiments have been conducted. No code has been trained. No physical tests have been performed.

1.4 Contributions

The thesis makes eight contributions.

1. Identification. A proof that the compatibility contrast K_ij identifies the pure interaction γ_ij under modularity (Theorem 4.1).
2. Reversal. A proof that independent recombination R2 is structurally more novel than coupled recombination R1 (Theorem 5.2).
3. Feasibility. A union-bound feasibility bound on R2 under hard constraints (Theorem 5.3).
4. Separation. A calibration/test separation theorem resolving the circularity of the compatibility map (Theorem 6.1).
5. Minimax. A minimax lower bound on the estimation of K_ij (Theorem 6.2).
6. Sample complexity. A sample complexity bound for the four-outcome decision rule (Theorem 8.2).
7. Extraction. A consistency theorem for MTCU extraction via interventional boundary discovery (Theorem 7.1).
8. Programme. A structured implementation programme with ten proposals (Chapter 10).

1.5 Structure

Chapter 2 establishes the mathematical background. Chapter 3 defines the formal objects. Chapter 4 proves identifiability. Chapter 5 develops the recombination calculus. Chapter 6 treats estimation and separation. Chapter 7 addresses MTCU extraction. Chapter 8 develops the statistical decision theory. Chapter 9 specifies the algorithms. Chapter 10 states the implementation programme. Chapter 11 analyzes threats to validity. Chapter 12 concludes.

