Chapter 2 — Mathematical Background

2.1 Structural causal models

Definition 2.1 (Structural causal model). A structural causal model (SCM) is a tuple \mathcal{M} = (V, U, F, P_U) where V is a finite set of endogenous variables, U is a finite set of exogenous variables, F = \{f_V : V \in V\} is a set of structural functions, and P_U is a distribution over U. Each endogenous variable is determined by

V = f_V(\mathrm{pa}(V), U_V)

where \mathrm{pa}(V) \subseteq V \setminus \{V\} are the parents of V in the causal graph \mathcal{G}.

Definition 2.2 (Intervention). For a subset X \subseteq V and values x, the intervention do(X = x) replaces the structural functions f_X with constants x, leaving all other functions unchanged. The interventional distribution is denoted P(\cdot \mid do(X = x)).

Definition 2.3 (Causal graph). The causal graph \mathcal{G} = (V, E) is the directed graph with edges V' \to V whenever V' \in \mathrm{pa}(V). We assume \mathcal{G} is acyclic.

2.2 Identification assumptions

Assumption 2.1 (Modularity / Independent Causal Mechanisms). For disjoint subsets A, B \subseteq V, intervening on A does not alter the structural functions f_B. Equivalently, the conditional distribution P(B \mid \mathrm{pa}(B)) is invariant under interventions on V \setminus (B \cup \mathrm{pa}(B)).

Assumption 2.2 (Positivity). For all interventions considered, 0 < P(X = x) < 1 under the intervention distribution.

Assumption 2.3 (Consistency). For any unit and any intervention do(X = x), the observed outcome equals the potential outcome Y(x).

Assumption 2.4 (SUTVA). The outcome for a unit depends only on its own treatment assignment, not on the assignments of other units.

2.3 The difference-in-differences estimator

Definition 2.4 (Two-way DiD). For a binary treatment T \in \{0, 1\}, a binary group G \in \{0, 1\}, and outcome Y, the difference-in-differences estimator is

\hat{\tau}_{\mathrm{DiD}} = \big(\bar{Y}_{11} - \bar{Y}_{10}\big) - \big(\bar{Y}_{01} - \bar{Y}_{00}\big)

where \bar{Y}_{tg} is the sample mean of Y in group g under treatment t.

Definition 2.5 (Factorial DiD). For two binary factors A, B \in \{0, 1\} and outcome Y, the factorial DiD estimator of interaction is

\hat{\tau}_{\mathrm{FDID}} = \big(\bar{Y}_{11} - \bar{Y}_{10}\big) - \big(\bar{Y}_{01} - \bar{Y}_{00}\big)

where \bar{Y}_{ab} is the sample mean under A = a, B = b.

Proposition 2.1 (Category V). Under the factorial parallel-trends assumption and Assumptions 2.1–2.4, the FDID estimator identifies the average causal interaction.

2.4 Survival analysis

Definition 2.6 (Survival function). For a non-negative random variable T, the survival function is S(t) = P(T > t).

Definition 2.7 (Censoring). Let C be a censoring time. The observed outcome is T^* = \min(T, C) with censoring indicator \Delta = \mathbb{1}\{T \leq C\}.

Definition 2.8 (Kaplan–Meier estimator). For distinct event times t_1 < t_2 < \cdots,

\hat{S}(t) = \prod_{j : t_j \leq t} \left(1 - \frac{d_j}{n_j}\right)

where d_j is the number of events at t_j and n_j is the number at risk.

Definition 2.9 (Restricted mean survival time). For a budget C,

\mathrm{RMST} = \int_0^C S(t) \, dt.

Theorem 2.1 (Log-rank power — Category V). For the comparison of two groups with hazard ratio e^\theta, the log-rank test has power 1 - \beta at level \alpha when the number of events D satisfies

D \geq \frac{4(z_{1-\alpha/2} + z_{1-\beta})^2}{\theta^2}.

2.5 Concentration and bootstrap

Theorem 2.2 (Hoeffding). For independent bounded random variables X_i \in [a_i, b_i],

P\left(\left|\sum_i X_i - \mathbb{E}\left[\sum_i X_i\right]\right| \geq t\right) \leq 2\exp\left(-\frac{2t^2}{\sum_i (b_i - a_i)^2}\right).

Theorem 2.3 (Bernstein). For independent zero-mean random variables with |X_i| \leq M and variance \sigma_i^2,

P\left(\left|\sum_i X_i\right| \geq t\right) \leq 2\exp\left(-\frac{t^2/2}{\sum_i \sigma_i^2 + Mt/3}\right).

Definition 2.10 (Bootstrap standard error). For a statistic \hat{\theta} estimated on n samples, the bootstrap standard error is the standard deviation of \hat{\theta} across B resamples drawn with replacement.

Proposition 2.2 (Category V). Under regularity conditions, the bootstrap standard error is a consistent estimator of the asymptotic standard error.

