Chapter 3 — Formal Objects

3.1 The perceptual genome

Definition 3.1 (Perceptual genome). A perceptual genome is a sequence P = (f_1, \ldots, f_n) where each filter is

f_i = (f_c^{(i)}, \mathrm{bw}^{(i)}, g^{(i)}, \mathrm{order}^{(i)})

with centre frequency f_c^{(i)}, bandwidth \mathrm{bw}^{(i)}, gain g^{(i)}, and order \mathrm{order}^{(i)}.

Definition 3.2 (Spectral coverage). The spectral coverage of a filter bank P is

\Phi(P) = \left| \bigcup_{i=1}^n [f_c^{(i)} - \mathrm{bw}^{(i)}/2, f_c^{(i)} + \mathrm{bw}^{(i)}/2] \right|

weighted by gain.

Definition 3.3 (Perceptual distillation). A distillation is a map D : P \mapsto P' \subseteq P such that \Phi(P') \geq (1 - \varepsilon) \Phi(P) for a pre-specified tolerance \varepsilon > 0.

3.2 The cognitive genome

Definition 3.4 (Cognitive genome). A cognitive genome is a tuple C = (A, M, R, \Pi) where A is an architecture graph, M = \{m_1, \ldots, m_k\} is a set of MTCUs, R is a set of memory rules, and \Pi is a set of procedures.

Definition 3.5 (Genotype–phenotype map). The map \Gamma : \mathcal{G} \to \mathcal{P} associates a genome G with a phenotype (agent behaviour) \Gamma(G). The map is not assumed injective, surjective, or deterministic.

3.3 The Minimal Transmissible Cognitive Unit

Definition 3.6 (MTCU). A cognitive component m \subseteq A is an MTCU if and only if:

1. (Causal minimality) For every proper subgraph m' \subsetneq m, \mathrm{IC}(m, m'; Y) \neq 0 on the calibration distribution.
2. (Transmissibility) m is representable as (\theta_m, \mathrm{topo}_m) with \theta_m containing no controller weights.
3. (Measurability) S_m = \mathbb{E}[Y \mid do(m \text{ active})] - \mathbb{E}[Y \mid do(m \text{ ablated})] is estimable by interventional contrast.
4. (Composability) m combines with other MTCUs under the hard constraints of Definition 5.1.

Remark 3.1. Criterion 1 operationalizes minimality via causal ablation. This is testable: if a subcomponent m' has no effect on Y under intervention, m is not minimal.

3.4 The joint genome and linkage

Definition 3.7 (Joint genome). A joint genome is a triple G = (P, C, L) where P is a perceptual genome, C is a cognitive genome, and L : P \to C is a linkage map assigning each perceptual component a cognitive counterpart.

Definition 3.8 (Linkage map). The linkage map L is a function L : \{1, \ldots, n\} \to \{1, \ldots, k\} such that L(i) = j means perceptual filter f_i is associated with MTCU m_j.

3.5 The compatibility contrast

Definition 3.9 (Compatibility contrast). For an MTCU m_i and a perceptual block P_j,

K_{ij} = \mathbb{E}[Y \mid do(P_j, m_i)] - \mathbb{E}[Y \mid do(P_j, m_0)] - \mathbb{E}[Y \mid do(P_0, m_i)] + \mathbb{E}[Y \mid do(P_0, m_0)]

where P_0, m_0 are pre-specified ablation references.

Definition 3.10 (Weighted compatibility map). The compatibility map is

L_{ij} = \sigma\!\left(\frac{K_{ij}}{T \cdot \widehat{\mathrm{SE}}(K_{ij})}\right)

where \sigma(z) = (1 + e^{-z})^{-1}, T > 0 is a temperature parameter determined only on calibration data, and \widehat{\mathrm{SE}}(K_{ij}) is a bootstrap standard error.

