Chapter 4 — Identifiability Theory

4.1 The additive-interactive model

Definition 4.1 (Additive-interactive decomposition). The expected potential outcome under joint intervention admits the decomposition

\mathbb{E}[Y \mid do(P_j, m_i)] = \mu_0 + \alpha_j + \beta_i + \gamma_{ij}

where \mu_0 = \mathbb{E}[Y \mid do(P_0, m_0)], \alpha_j = \mathbb{E}[Y \mid do(P_j, m_0)] - \mu_0, \beta_i = \mathbb{E}[Y \mid do(P_0, m_i)] - \mu_0, and \gamma_{ij} is the pure interaction.

Remark 4.1. The additive-interactive decomposition is a modeling assumption. It is not required for K_{ij} to be identifiable; it is required for K_{ij} to be interpreted as the pure interaction \gamma_{ij}.

4.2 The identification theorem

Theorem 4.1 (Identification — Category M, validated by V). Under Assumptions 2.1 (modularity), 2.2 (positivity), 2.3 (consistency), and 2.4 (SUTVA), the compatibility contrast K_{ij} identifies the pure interaction \gamma_{ij} on the chosen outcome scale.

Proof. The four interventional expectations are identifiable under Assumptions 2.2–2.4. Under Assumption 2.1, the main effects \alpha_j and \beta_i are invariant under joint intervention, so the decomposition of Definition 4.1 is valid. Substituting into the four-term contrast:

\begin{aligned}
K_{ij} &= (\mu_0 + \alpha_j + \beta_i + \gamma_{ij}) - (\mu_0 + \alpha_j) - (\mu_0 + \beta_i) + \mu_0 \\
&= \mu_0 + \alpha_j + \beta_i + \gamma_{ij} - \mu_0 - \alpha_j - \mu_0 - \beta_i + \mu_0 \\
&= \gamma_{ij}.
\end{aligned}

The baseline terms (\mu_0 - \mu_0 - \mu_0 + \mu_0) sum to zero. The perceptual main effects (\alpha_j - \alpha_j) cancel. The cognitive main effects (\beta_i - \beta_i) cancel. What remains is \gamma_{ij}. \square

Corollary 4.1. If Assumption 2.1 fails, K_{ij} is not identifiable from interventional data alone. A causal discovery step is required.

Corollary 4.2. If Assumption 2.2 fails (zero-probability conditioning), the interventional distributions are undefined and K_{ij} is not estimable.

4.3 Scale dependence

Proposition 4.1 (Scale dependence — Category M). The sign of K_{ij} depends on the outcome scale.

Proof. Let Y be a performance to maximise and Y' = -Y be an equivalent loss. Then

K'_{ij} = \mathbb{E}[-Y \mid do(P_j, m_i)] - \mathbb{E}[-Y \mid do(P_j, m_0)] - \mathbb{E}[-Y \mid do(P_0, m_i)] + \mathbb{E}[-Y \mid do(P_0, m_0)] = -K_{ij}.

The sign reverses. \square

Corollary 4.3. The primary outcome must be pre-registered on a scale where K_{ij} > 0 unambiguously indicates positive interaction. We recommend the probability of reaching a success threshold within a fixed training budget, which is maximised.

