Chapter 5 — The Recombination Calculus

5.1 Constraint sets

Definition 5.1 (Hard constraints). A recombined genome G_R = (P_R, C_R, L_R) is hard-valid if:

· (H1) Interface compatibility: \dim(P_R^j) = \dim(m_R^j) for all j.
· (H2) No orphans or duplicates: Every m \in C_R appears exactly once; no m appears without its P-counterpart.
· (H3) Architecture validity: A_R is acyclic and connected.
· (H4) No controller leakage: No parameter from either parent's controller is imported.

Definition 5.2 (Soft constraints for R1). G_R is soft-valid for R1 if it is hard-valid and for every j,

(P_R^j, C_R^j) \in \{(P_A^j, C_A^j), (P_B^j, C_B^j)\}.

Definition 5.3 (Soft constraints for R2). G_R is soft-valid for R2 if it is hard-valid. No functional compatibility is required.

5.2 The recombination operators

Definition 5.4 (R1 — coupled recombination). For parents G_A = (P_A, C_A, L_A) and G_B = (P_B, C_B, L_B), partition P_A and P_B into k blocks each. For each j, draw s_j \sim \mathrm{Uniform}(\{A, B\}). Set P_R^j = P_{s_j}^j and C_R^j = L_{s_j}(P_{s_j}^j).

Definition 5.5 (R2 — independent recombination). With the same partition, for each j draw s_j^P \sim \mathrm{Uniform}(\{A, B\}) and s_j^C \sim \mathrm{Uniform}(\{A, B\}) independently. Set P_R^j = P_{s_j^P}^j and C_R^j = C_{s_j^C}^j.

Definition 5.6 (R3 — no recombination). Parental structures are retained without exchange.

5.3 The R1/R2 reversal

Definition 5.7 (Novelty set). For a genome G, let \mathcal{N}(G) denote the set of MTCU-context pairs (P_i, C_j) present in G.

Theorem 5.1 (Structural novelty ordering — Category M). For any R1 recombination G_{R1} and any R2 recombination G_{R2} with the same parental blocks,

|\mathcal{N}(G_{R1}) \setminus (\mathcal{N}(G_A) \cup \mathcal{N}(G_B))| \leq |\mathcal{N}(G_{R2}) \setminus (\mathcal{N}(G_A) \cup \mathcal{N}(G_B))|

with strict inequality whenever at least one block is exchanged.

Proof. For R1, each (P_R^j, C_R^j) equals (P_A^j, C_A^j) or (P_B^j, C_B^j). Both are in \mathcal{N}(G_A) \cup \mathcal{N}(G_B). Hence the left-hand side is 0.

For R2, suppose s_j^P = A and s_j^C = B for some j, with j not identical in both parents. Then (P_A^j, C_B^j) \in \mathcal{N}(G_{R2}). Is this pair in \mathcal{N}(G_A) \cup \mathcal{N}(G_B)? \mathcal{N}(G_A) contains (P_A^j, C_A^j) for all j. \mathcal{N}(G_B) contains (P_B^j, C_B^j) for all j. The pair (P_A^j, C_B^j) is in neither unless A and B happen to share the same components at block j. Under the assumption that at least one block is exchanged, the right-hand side is at least 1. \square

Corollary 5.1. The decisive test for recombination proper is R2 vs \max(R1, R3). R1 tests modularity, not recombination.

5.4 Feasibility of R2

Theorem 5.2 (R2 feasibility bound — Category M). Let q be the probability that a random R2 recombination satisfies the hard constraints. If the soft constraints applicable to R2 are exactly the hard constraints, then

q \geq 1 - \sum_{j=1}^{k} (1 - p_{\mathrm{iface}}^j) - P(\text{cycle}) - P(\text{orphan})

where p_{\mathrm{iface}}^j is the probability that block j's interface dimensions match.

Proof. Let E_1, \ldots, E_k be the events that block j's interface mismatches, E_{\mathrm{cycle}} the event of a cycle, and E_{\mathrm{orphan}} the event of an orphan or duplicate. The rejection event is E_1 \cup \cdots \cup E_k \cup E_{\mathrm{cycle}} \cup E_{\mathrm{orphan}}. By the union bound,

P(\text{reject}) \leq \sum_{j=1}^k P(E_j) + P(E_{\mathrm{cycle}}) + P(E_{\mathrm{orphan}}).

Since q = 1 - P(\text{reject}) and P(E_j) = 1 - p_{\mathrm{iface}}^j, the bound follows. \square

Corollary 5.2. If soft constraints for R2 include functional compatibility (high L_{ij}), then q \approx P(L_{ij} > \tau) for a random pair, which may be near zero. This justifies hard-constraint-only R2.

5.5 Expected number of R2 draws

Proposition 5.1 (Category M). The expected number of R2 draws to obtain one valid descendant is 1/q, where q is the feasibility probability.

Proof. Each draw is an independent Bernoulli trial with success probability q. The number of trials to first success follows a geometric distribution with mean 1/q. \square

Corollary 5.3. If q = 0.10, the expected number of draws per valid descendant is 10. If q < 0.01, R2 is practically infeasible in the tested domain.

