Chapter 6 — Estimation and Separation

6.1 The circularity problem

Problem 6.1. If L is estimated on a task set \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{cal}} and used on the same set, the comparison between R1 and R2 is tautological: R1 selects high-K_{ij} pairs, R2 ignores them. The scientific content requires separation.

6.2 The separation theorem

Theorem 6.1 (Calibration/test separation — Category M). Let \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{cal}} and \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{test}} be disjoint task sets. Let \hat{L} be the compatibility map estimated on \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{cal}}. Then:

1. The performance of \hat{L} on \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{cal}} is an upward-biased estimate of its out-of-sample performance, with bias O(|\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{cal}}|^{-1/2}).
2. The performance of \hat{L} on \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{test}} is an unbiased estimate of the predictive information of the compatibility structure, provided \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{test}} and \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{cal}} are drawn from the same task distribution.

Proof. (1) \hat{L} is chosen to maximise calibration performance. By the optimism of empirical risk minimisation, \mathbb{E}[\mathrm{Perf}_{\mathrm{cal}}(\hat{L})] \geq \mathbb{E}[\mathrm{Perf}(\hat{L})]. The bias rate O(|\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{cal}}|^{-1/2}) follows from standard uniform convergence arguments (Hoeffding's inequality applied to the performance functional). (2) Since \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{test}} is independent of \hat{L}, \mathbb{E}[\mathrm{Perf}_{\mathrm{test}}(\hat{L})] = \mathbb{E}[\mathrm{Perf}(\hat{L})] under the task distribution. \square

6.3 Minimax lower bound

Theorem 6.2 (Minimax lower bound — Category M). Let K_{ij} be the compatibility contrast, estimated from n i.i.d. samples. Under the assumption that the interventional outcomes are bounded in [0, 1], the minimax risk satisfies

\inf_{\hat{K}} \sup_{K \in \mathcal{K}} \mathbb{E}\left[(\hat{K} - K)^2\right] \geq \frac{c}{n}

for a universal constant c > 0.

Proof. Apply Le Cam's method. Consider two hypotheses: K = 0 (no interaction) and K = \delta (interaction). The Kullback–Leibler divergence between the corresponding interventional distributions is O(n \delta^2) under bounded outcomes. By Le Cam's two-point argument, the minimax risk is at least

\frac{\delta^2}{2} \left(1 - \sqrt{\frac{n \delta^2}{2}}\right)

for \delta = c/\sqrt{n}. Optimising over \delta gives the c/n rate. \square

Corollary 6.1. The bootstrap standard error \widehat{\mathrm{SE}}(K_{ij}) scales as O(n^{-1/2}), so the normalised statistic K_{ij} / \widehat{\mathrm{SE}}(K_{ij}) is O(\sqrt{n}) under the alternative.

6.4 The temperature parameter

Definition 6.1 (Temperature). The temperature T > 0 controls the sharpness of the sigmoid map. As T \to \infty, L_{ij} \to 0.5. As T \to 0, L_{ij} \to \mathbb{1}\{K_{ij} > 0\}.

Proposition 6.1 (Category M). The temperature T must be determined only on \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{cal}}. Any optimisation of T on \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{test}} invalidates Theorem 6.1.

Proof. Direct from the proof of Theorem 6.1: if T depends on \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{test}}, then \hat{L} depends on \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{test}}, and the independence required for unbiasedness fails. \square

