Chapter 7 — MTCU Extraction

7.1 Interventional boundary discovery

Definition 7.1 (Causal sphere of influence). The causal sphere of influence is

O_{\mathrm{causal}} = \{o \in O : \exists a, a' \text{ such that } P(o \mid do(a)) \neq P(o \mid do(a'))\}.

Algorithm 7.1 (Interventional Boundary Discovery).

```
Input: Trained agent π, environment E, observation dims O, budget N
Output: O_causal

1. Randomize actions: a ~ Uniform(A)
2. For each o ∈ O:
   a. Collect N samples under randomized actions
   b. Two-sample test (KS or Mann-Whitney) between o | a and o | a'
   c. FDR correction (Benjamini–Hochberg)
   d. If significant: o ∈ O_causal
3. Return O_causal
```

Theorem 7.1 (IBD consistency — Category M, validated by V). Under the assumption that the agent's action channel provides randomized interventions and that observation dimensions are independent, Algorithm 7.1 identifies O_{\mathrm{causal}} with probability tending to 1 as N \to \infty.

Proof. Let o \in O_{\mathrm{causal}}. By Definition 7.1, there exist a, a' with P(o \mid do(a)) \neq P(o \mid do(a')). The two-sample test is consistent, so it rejects the null with probability tending to 1 as N \to \infty. The Benjamini–Hochberg procedure controls FDR and, under the alternative, has power tending to 1. Hence o is included. Conversely, if o \notin O_{\mathrm{causal}}, then P(o \mid do(a)) = P(o \mid do(a')) for all a, a', so the test does not reject and o is excluded. \square

7.2 Cognitive subgraph extraction

Definition 7.2 (Causal downstream subgraph). A subgraph m \subseteq A is causally downstream of O_{\mathrm{causal}} if ablating O_{\mathrm{non-causal}} does not change the activations of m.

Algorithm 7.2 (MTCU Extraction).

```
Input: Trained agent π, environment E, observation dims O, budget N
Output: MTCU set M

1. O_causal = IBD(π, E, O, N)
2. For each candidate subgraph m ⊆ A:
   a. Ablate O_non-causal
   b. If m's activations unchanged:
      i. Recurse on m's submodules to find minimal m
      ii. Verify Definition 3.6 criteria 1–4
      iii. If all pass: add m to M
3. Return M
```

Theorem 7.2 (Extraction consistency — Category M). Under the assumptions of Theorem 7.1 and the faithfulness of the causal graph, Algorithm 7.2 returns the set of minimal subgraphs satisfying Definition 3.6 with probability tending to 1 as N \to \infty.

Proof. By Theorem 7.1, O_{\mathrm{causal}} is identified. The downstream subgraph test is a conditional independence test, which is consistent under faithfulness. Recursion terminates because A is finite and acyclic. Definition 3.6 criteria 1–4 are verified by interventional contrasts, which are consistent. \square

