Chapter 8 — Statistical Decision Theory

8.1 Primary outcome

Definition 8.1 (Primary estimand). The primary estimand is the restricted mean survival time

\mathrm{RMST}_g = \int_0^C S_g(t) \, dt

where S_g is the survival function for group g and C is the training budget.

8.2 Falsification criteria

Definition 8.2 (Minimal relevant effect). The minimal relevant effect is \delta^* = 0.10 (10 percentage points in success probability within budget).

Definition 8.3 (Equivalence bound). The equivalence bound is \delta_{\mathrm{equiv}} = 0.05.

Criterion 8.1 (Primary falsification). H-FRAME_{\mathrm{strong}} is refuted if

\mathrm{RMST}_{R2} - \max(\mathrm{RMST}_{R1}, \mathrm{RMST}_{R3}) < \delta^*

with a 95% confidence interval for the difference excluding \delta^*.

Criterion 8.2 (Coupled falsification). H-FRAME_{\mathrm{coupled}} is refuted if

\Delta_{\mathrm{pred}} = \mathrm{Perf}_{\mathrm{test}}(L) - \mathrm{Perf}_{\mathrm{test}}(L_{\mathrm{perm}}) \leq 0

or if R2's advantage collapses at equal acceptance rate.

8.3 Sample complexity

Theorem 8.1 (Sample complexity of the decision — Category M). To distinguish K_{ij} = 0 from K_{ij} = \delta with power 1 - \beta at level \alpha, the required number of samples per interventional condition is

n = O\left(\frac{\sigma^2 (z_{1-\alpha/2} + z_{1-\beta})^2}{\delta^2}\right)

where \sigma^2 is the variance of the outcome.

Proof. The two-sample test statistic has variance 2\sigma^2 / n. The required separation is (z_{1-\alpha/2} + z_{1-\beta}) \sqrt{2\sigma^2 / n}. Setting this equal to \delta and solving for n gives the result. \square

8.4 The four-outcome decision rule

Outcome Condition
Supported \mathrm{RMST}_{R2} > \max(\mathrm{RMST}_{R1}, \mathrm{RMST}_{R3}) + \delta^* and \Delta_{\mathrm{pred}} > 0
Partially supported \mathrm{RMST}_{R2} > \max(\mathrm{RMST}_{R1}, \mathrm{RMST}_{R3}) + \delta^* but \Delta_{\mathrm{pred}} \leq 0
Refuted \(
Inconclusive Neither supported nor refuted with sufficient power

Theorem 8.2 (Decision consistency — Category M). Under the sample complexity of Theorem 8.1, the four-outcome decision rule selects the correct outcome with probability tending to 1 as n \to \infty.

Proof. The primary comparison is a two-sample test with power 1 - \beta by Theorem 8.1. The predictive transfer test is a two-sample test on independent data by Theorem 6.1. The equivalence test is a non-inferiority test with the same sample complexity. As n \to \infty, all tests are consistent. \square

