Appendix A — Proof of Theorem 4.1 (Identification of the Compatibility Contrast)

A.1 Formal setup

Let \mathcal{M} = (V, U, F, P_U) be a structural causal model with endogenous variables V, exogenous variables U, structural functions F, and exogenous distribution P_U. Let P_j \subseteq V be a perceptual block and m_i \subseteq V an MTCU. Let Y \in V be the outcome. Define the interventional distribution under do(P_j = p_j, m_i = m_i^{\mathrm{act}}) as P(\cdot \mid do(P_j, m_i)).

A.2 Assumptions

Assumption A.1 (Modularity / Independent Causal Mechanisms). For disjoint subsets A, B \subseteq V, intervening on A does not alter the structural functions f_B. Equivalently, the conditional distribution P(B \mid \mathrm{pa}(B)) is invariant under interventions on V \setminus (B \cup \mathrm{pa}(B)). This is the standard modularity assumption of Pearl (2009) and is equivalent to the factorization and modularity property implicit in Single World Intervention Graphs (SWIGs), proved by Richardson and Robins (2013) and Shpitser et al. (2022). The intervention do(x) does not alter the equations for variables not subject to intervention, an assumption known as autonomy or modularity.

Assumption A.2 (Positivity). For all interventions considered, 0 < P(P_j = p_j) < 1 and 0 < P(m_i = m_i^{\mathrm{act}}) < 1 under the intervention distribution.

Assumption A.3 (Consistency). For any unit and any intervention do(X = x), the observed outcome equals the potential outcome Y(x).

Assumption A.4 (SUTVA). The outcome for a unit depends only on its own treatment assignment, not on the assignments of other units.

A.3 The additive-interactive decomposition

Definition A.1. The expected potential outcome under joint intervention admits the decomposition

\mathbb{E}[Y \mid do(P_j, m_i)] = \mu_0 + \alpha_j + \beta_i + \gamma_{ij}

where

\mu_0 = \mathbb{E}[Y \mid do(P_0, m_0)], \quad
\alpha_j = \mathbb{E}[Y \mid do(P_j, m_0)] - \mu_0, \quad
\beta_i = \mathbb{E}[Y \mid do(P_0, m_i)] - \mu_0

and \gamma_{ij} is the pure interaction.

Remark A.1. This decomposition is a modeling assumption. It is not required for K_{ij} to be identifiable; it is required for K_{ij} to be interpreted as the pure interaction \gamma_{ij}. The decomposition is analogous to the factorial structure in factorial difference-in-differences (FDID), where the DID estimator identifies effect modification but not causal moderation under canonical assumptions. Identifying causal moderation requires an additional factorial parallel trends assumption, that is, mean independence between the baseline factor and potential outcome trends. In H-FRAME, modularity plays the role of the factorial parallel trends assumption.

A.4 Proof

Theorem A.1 (Identification). Under Assumptions A.1–A.4, the compatibility contrast K_{ij} identifies the pure interaction \gamma_{ij}.

Proof. The four interventional expectations are identifiable under Assumptions A.2–A.4. Under Assumption A.1, the main effects \alpha_j and \beta_i are invariant under joint intervention, so the decomposition of Definition A.1 is valid. Substituting into the four-term contrast:

\begin{aligned}
K_{ij} &= \mathbb{E}[Y \mid do(P_j, m_i)] - \mathbb{E}[Y \mid do(P_j, m_0)] - \mathbb{E}[Y \mid do(P_0, m_i)] + \mathbb{E}[Y \mid do(P_0, m_0)] \\
&= (\mu_0 + \alpha_j + \beta_i + \gamma_{ij}) - (\mu_0 + \alpha_j) - (\mu_0 + \beta_i) + \mu_0 \\
&= \mu_0 + \alpha_j + \beta_i + \gamma_{ij} - \mu_0 - \alpha_j - \mu_0 - \beta_i + \mu_0 \\
&= \gamma_{ij}.
\end{aligned}

The baseline terms (\mu_0 - \mu_0 - \mu_0 + \mu_0) sum to zero. The perceptual main effects (\alpha_j - \alpha_j) cancel. The cognitive main effects (\beta_i - \beta_i) cancel. What remains is \gamma_{ij}. \square

Corollary A.1. If Assumption A.1 fails, K_{ij} is not identifiable from interventional data alone. A causal discovery step is required.

Corollary A.2. If Assumption A.2 fails (zero-probability conditioning), the interventional distributions are undefined and K_{ij} is not estimable.

A.5 Relation to factorial difference-in-differences

The FDID framework of Xu, Zhao, and Ding (2026) formalizes four estimands in the FDID setting: the effect modification of the baseline factor G, the causal moderation, and others. Under canonical no anticipation and parallel trends assumptions, the DID estimator identifies only effect modification; identifying causal moderation requires an additional factorial parallel trends assumption. The H-FRAME compatibility contrast K_{ij} plays the role of the causal moderation estimand, and modularity (Assumption A.1) plays the role of the factorial parallel trends assumption. The algebraic cancellation in the proof above is the H-FRAME analogue of the FDID identification result.

