Appendix B — Proof of Theorem 5.1 (Structural Novelty Ordering)

B.1 Definitions

Definition B.1 (Novelty set). For a genome G, let \mathcal{N}(G) denote the set of MTCU-context pairs (P_i, C_j) present in G.

Definition B.2 (R1 — coupled recombination). For parents G_A = (P_A, C_A, L_A) and G_B = (P_B, C_B, L_B), partition P_A and P_B into k blocks each. For each j, draw s_j \sim \mathrm{Uniform}(\{A, B\}). Set P_R^j = P_{s_j}^j and C_R^j = L_{s_j}(P_{s_j}^j).

Definition B.3 (R2 — independent recombination). With the same partition, for each j draw s_j^P \sim \mathrm{Uniform}(\{A, B\}) and s_j^C \sim \mathrm{Uniform}(\{A, B\}) independently. Set P_R^j = P_{s_j^P}^j and C_R^j = C_{s_j^C}^j.

B.2 Proof

Theorem B.1 (Structural novelty ordering). For any R1 recombination G_{R1} and any R2 recombination G_{R2} with the same parental blocks,

|\mathcal{N}(G_{R1}) \setminus (\mathcal{N}(G_A) \cup \mathcal{N}(G_B))| \leq |\mathcal{N}(G_{R2}) \setminus (\mathcal{N}(G_A) \cup \mathcal{N}(G_B))|

with strict inequality whenever at least one block is exchanged.

Proof. For R1, each (P_R^j, C_R^j) equals (P_A^j, C_A^j) or (P_B^j, C_B^j). Both are in \mathcal{N}(G_A) \cup \mathcal{N}(G_B). Hence the left-hand side is 0.

For R2, suppose s_j^P = A and s_j^C = B for some j, with j not identical in both parents. Then (P_A^j, C_B^j) \in \mathcal{N}(G_{R2}). Is this pair in \mathcal{N}(G_A) \cup \mathcal{N}(G_B)? \mathcal{N}(G_A) contains (P_A^j, C_A^j) for all j. \mathcal{N}(G_B) contains (P_B^j, C_B^j) for all j. The pair (P_A^j, C_B^j) is in neither unless A and B happen to share the same components at block j. Under the assumption that at least one block is exchanged, the right-hand side is at least 1. \square

Corollary B.1. The decisive test for recombination proper is R2 vs \max(R1, R3). R1 tests modularity, not recombination.

B.3 Interpretation

The R1/R2 reversal is a formal result of the integration of AISE and AC-DNA. AISE does not distinguish coupled from independent recombination, and AC-DNA does not specify whether associations are preserved. The reversal is not derivable from either framework alone.

