Appendix C — Proof of Theorem 5.2 (R2 Feasibility Bound)

C.1 Hard constraints

Definition C.1 (Hard constraints). A recombined genome G_R = (P_R, C_R, L_R) is hard-valid if:

· (H1) Interface compatibility: \dim(P_R^j) = \dim(m_R^j) for all j.
· (H2) No orphans or duplicates: Every m \in C_R appears exactly once; no m appears without its P-counterpart.
· (H3) Architecture validity: A_R is acyclic and connected.
· (H4) No controller leakage: No parameter from either parent's controller is imported.

C.2 Proof

Theorem C.1 (R2 feasibility bound). Let q be the probability that a random R2 recombination satisfies the hard constraints. If the soft constraints applicable to R2 are exactly the hard constraints, then

q \geq 1 - \sum_{j=1}^{k} (1 - p_{\mathrm{iface}}^j) - P(\text{cycle}) - P(\text{orphan})

where p_{\mathrm{iface}}^j is the probability that block j's interface dimensions match.

Proof. Let E_1, \ldots, E_k be the events that block j's interface mismatches, E_{\mathrm{cycle}} the event of a cycle, and E_{\mathrm{orphan}} the event of an orphan or duplicate. The rejection event is E_1 \cup \cdots \cup E_k \cup E_{\mathrm{cycle}} \cup E_{\mathrm{orphan}}. By the union bound,

P(\text{reject}) \leq \sum_{j=1}^k P(E_j) + P(E_{\mathrm{cycle}}) + P(E_{\mathrm{orphan}}).

Since q = 1 - P(\text{reject}) and P(E_j) = 1 - p_{\mathrm{iface}}^j, the bound follows. \square

C.3 Expected number of draws

Proposition C.1. The expected number of R2 draws to obtain one valid descendant is 1/q, where q is the feasibility probability.

Proof. Each draw is an independent Bernoulli trial with success probability q. The number of trials to first success follows a geometric distribution with mean 1/q. \square

Corollary C.1. If q = 0.10, the expected number of draws per valid descendant is 10. If q < 0.01, R2 is practically infeasible in the tested domain.

C.4 Relation to modular linkage preservation

The modularity-based linkage model for neuroevolution (Qiao & Gallagher, GECCO '23) establishes that disregarding inter-dependency between parameters is a primary source of disruption of recombination between neural networks. The R2 feasibility bound formalizes the cost of disregarding this dependency: the rejection rate 1 - q is the price of exploratory novelty.

