Appendix D — Proof of Theorem 6.1 (Calibration/Test Separation)

D.1 Setup

Let \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{cal}} and \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{test}} be disjoint task sets. Let \hat{L} be the compatibility map estimated on \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{cal}}. Let \mathrm{Perf}(\hat{L}) denote the performance of the map on a task set.

D.2 Proof

Theorem D.1 (Calibration/test separation).

1. The performance of \hat{L} on \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{cal}} is an upward-biased estimate of its out-of-sample performance, with bias O(|\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{cal}}|^{-1/2}).
2. The performance of \hat{L} on \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{test}} is an unbiased estimate of the predictive information of the compatibility structure, provided \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{test}} and \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{cal}} are drawn from the same task distribution.

Proof. (1) \hat{L} is chosen to maximise calibration performance. By the optimism of empirical risk minimisation, \mathbb{E}[\mathrm{Perf}_{\mathrm{cal}}(\hat{L})] \geq \mathbb{E}[\mathrm{Perf}(\hat{L})]. The bias rate O(|\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{cal}}|^{-1/2}) follows from standard uniform convergence arguments. Specifically, for a function class \mathcal{L} with VC dimension d,

\sup_{L \in \mathcal{L}} |\mathrm{Perf}_{\mathrm{cal}}(L) - \mathrm{Perf}(L)| = O_P\left(\sqrt{\frac{d}{|\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{cal}}|}}\right)

by the Vapnik–Chervonenkis theorem. Since \hat{L} is selected from \mathcal{L}, the optimism is bounded by this uniform deviation.

(2) Since \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{test}} is independent of \hat{L},

\mathbb{E}[\mathrm{Perf}_{\mathrm{test}}(\hat{L})] = \mathbb{E}[\mathrm{Perf}(\hat{L})]

under the task distribution. \square

D.3 Bootstrap consistency for the compatibility map

The bootstrap standard error \widehat{\mathrm{SE}}(K_{ij}) used in the weighted compatibility map L_{ij} = \sigma(K_{ij} / (T \cdot \widehat{\mathrm{SE}}(K_{ij}))) is consistent under the following conditions. General bootstrap consistency theory for double machine learning estimators (Liu & Fang, 2026) establishes that a bootstrap scheme where only the finite-dimensional parameter is recalculated in the bootstrap sample, with the nuisance estimand fixed from the original data, is theoretically sound because DML's orthogonality to the first-stage nuisance estimand ensures the source of the nuisance function is irrelevant. The centered bootstrap estimand converges to the correct limiting distribution in probability. For the H-FRAME compatibility contrast, the interventional contrast K_{ij} is a finite-dimensional parameter, and the bootstrap resampling of calibration tasks provides a consistent estimate of its standard error.

