Appendix E — Proof of Theorem 6.2 (Minimax Lower Bound)

E.1 Le Cam's two-point method

Theorem E.1 (Le Cam's two-point lemma). Let P_0 and P_1 be two probability distributions on a measurable space, and let \theta(P) be a parameter of interest. Then

\inf_{\hat{\theta}} \max_{j \in \{0, 1\}} \mathbb{E}_{P_j}[(\hat{\theta} - \theta(P_j))^2] \geq \frac{(\theta(P_1) - \theta(P_0))^2}{4} \left(1 - \mathrm{TV}(P_0, P_1)\right)

where \mathrm{TV}(P_0, P_1) = \frac{1}{2} \int |dP_0 - dP_1| is the total variation distance.

Proof. See Tsybakov (2009, Chapter 2). The reduction to two simple hypotheses is the standard two-point device used in minimax lower bounds. Convergence of the two-point local experiments in Le Cam's sense implies convergence of the optimal bounded-loss risk for the corresponding simple testing problem.

E.2 Proof of the minimax lower bound

Theorem E.2 (Minimax lower bound for K_{ij}). Let K_{ij} be the compatibility contrast, estimated from n i.i.d. samples. Under the assumption that the interventional outcomes are bounded in [0, 1], the minimax risk satisfies

\inf_{\hat{K}} \sup_{K \in \mathcal{K}} \mathbb{E}\left[(\hat{K} - K)^2\right] \geq \frac{c}{n}

for a universal constant c > 0.

Proof. Apply Le Cam's two-point method. Consider two hypotheses:

· H_0: K = 0 (no interaction).
· H_1: K = \delta (interaction of size \delta).

Under H_0, the interventional distributions satisfy \mathbb{E}[Y \mid do(P_j, m_i)] = \mu_0 + \alpha_j + \beta_i. Under H_1, \mathbb{E}[Y \mid do(P_j, m_i)] = \mu_0 + \alpha_j + \beta_i + \delta.

The Kullback–Leibler divergence between the corresponding interventional distributions is O(n \delta^2) under bounded outcomes. By Le Cam's two-point lemma,

\inf_{\hat{K}} \max_{j \in \{0, 1\}} \mathbb{E}_{P_j}[(\hat{K} - K_j)^2] \geq \frac{\delta^2}{4} \left(1 - \mathrm{TV}(P_0, P_1)\right).

Since \mathrm{TV}(P_0, P_1) \leq \sqrt{\frac{1}{2} \mathrm{KL}(P_0 \| P_1)}, and \mathrm{KL}(P_0 \| P_1) = O(n \delta^2), we have

\mathrm{TV}(P_0, P_1) \leq O(\delta \sqrt{n}).

For \delta = c/\sqrt{n}, the total variation is bounded by a constant less than 1, and the minimax risk is at least \Omega(1/n). Optimising over \delta gives the c/n rate. \square

Corollary E.1. The bootstrap standard error \widehat{\mathrm{SE}}(K_{ij}) scales as O(n^{-1/2}), so the normalised statistic K_{ij} / \widehat{\mathrm{SE}}(K_{ij}) is O(\sqrt{n}) under the alternative.

E.3 Relation to heterogeneous causal effect estimation

Minimax rates for heterogeneous causal effect estimation (Kallus, Mao, & Uehara, arXiv:2203.00837) derive lower bounds using a localized version of the method of fuzzy hypotheses, combining lower bound constructions for nonparametric regression and functional estimation. The primary strategy is to construct distributions that are similar enough to be statistically indistinguishable but for which the parameter of interest is maximally separated; this implies no estimator can have error uniformly smaller than this separation. The H-FRAME minimax lower bound is a two-point specialization of this general framework.

