Appendix F — Proof of Theorem 7.1 (IBD Consistency)

F.1 Setup

Let O = \{o_1, \ldots, o_d\} be the observation dimensions. Let A be the action space. Let O_{\mathrm{causal}} = \{o \in O : \exists a, a' \text{ such that } P(o \mid do(a)) \neq P(o \mid do(a'))\}.

F.2 Algorithm

Algorithm F.1 (Interventional Boundary Discovery).

```
Input: Trained agent π, environment E, observation dims O, budget N
Output: O_causal

1. Randomize actions: a ~ Uniform(A)
2. For each o ∈ O:
   a. Collect N samples under randomized actions
   b. Two-sample test (KS or Mann-Whitney) between o | a and o | a'
   c. FDR correction (Benjamini–Hochberg)
   d. If significant: o ∈ O_causal
3. Return O_causal
```

F.3 Proof

Theorem F.1 (IBD consistency). Under the assumption that the agent's action channel provides randomized interventions and that observation dimensions are independent, Algorithm F.1 identifies O_{\mathrm{causal}} with probability tending to 1 as N \to \infty.

Proof. Let o \in O_{\mathrm{causal}}. By definition, there exist a, a' with P(o \mid do(a)) \neq P(o \mid do(a')). The two-sample test (Kolmogorov–Smirnov or Mann–Whitney) is consistent, so it rejects the null with probability tending to 1 as N \to \infty. The Benjamini–Hochberg procedure controls FDR and, under the alternative, has power tending to 1. Hence o is included. Conversely, if o \notin O_{\mathrm{causal}}, then P(o \mid do(a)) = P(o \mid do(a')) for all a, a', so the test does not reject and o is excluded. \square

Remark F.1. The IBD procedure treats the agent's own action channel as a source of randomized interventions: randomizing actions implements an interventional contrast, and per-dimension two-sample tests with FDR correction produce a binary mask over observation dimensions. Across all settings, IBD achieves mean precision, recall, and F1 of 0.95, 0.93, and 0.94, indicating that FDR-controlled intervention testing rarely readmits the exogenous distractors that feature selection is meant to remove. Observational feature selection can actively select confounded distractors while discarding true causal dimensions, which motivates the interventional approach.

