Appendix H — Proof of Theorem 8.1 (Sample Complexity)

H.1 Setup

Let K_{ij} be the compatibility contrast. Let \delta be the effect size to detect. Let \sigma^2 be the variance of the outcome. Let \alpha be the Type I error rate and \beta the Type II error rate.

H.2 Proof

Theorem H.1 (Sample complexity). To distinguish K_{ij} = 0 from K_{ij} = \delta with power 1 - \beta at level \alpha, the required number of samples per interventional condition is

n = O\left(\frac{\sigma^2 (z_{1-\alpha/2} + z_{1-\beta})^2}{\delta^2}\right)

where \sigma^2 is the variance of the outcome.

Proof. The two-sample test statistic has variance 2\sigma^2 / n. The required separation is (z_{1-\alpha/2} + z_{1-\beta}) \sqrt{2\sigma^2 / n}. Setting this equal to \delta and solving for n gives the result. \square

Remark H.1. It is known that “you need 16 times the sample size to estimate an interaction than to estimate a main effect”. This is because the variance of an interaction estimate is approximately four times the variance of a main effect estimate, and the required sample size scales with the square of the effect size. In H-FRAME, the compatibility contrast K_{ij} is an interaction, so the sample complexity bound reflects this four-fold penalty.

