Appendix K — Mathematical Background

K.1 Structural causal models

Definition K.1 (SCM). A structural causal model is a tuple \mathcal{M} = (V, U, F, P_U) where V is a finite set of endogenous variables, U is a finite set of exogenous variables, F = \{f_V : V \in V\} is a set of structural functions, and P_U is a distribution over U. Each endogenous variable is determined by

V = f_V(\mathrm{pa}(V), U_V)

where \mathrm{pa}(V) \subseteq V \setminus \{V\} are the parents of V in the causal graph \mathcal{G}.

K.2 Modularity

Definition K.2 (Modularity). For disjoint subsets A, B \subseteq V, intervening on A does not alter the structural functions f_B. Equivalently, the conditional distribution P(B \mid \mathrm{pa}(B)) is invariant under interventions on V \setminus (B \cup \mathrm{pa}(B)). This is the standard modularity assumption of Pearl (2009), equivalent to the factorization and modularity property implicit in SWIGs.

K.3 Difference-in-differences

Definition K.3 (Two-way DiD). For a binary treatment T \in \{0, 1\}, a binary group G \in \{0, 1\}, and outcome Y, the difference-in-differences estimator is

\hat{\tau}_{\mathrm{DiD}} = \big(\bar{Y}_{11} - \bar{Y}_{10}\big) - \big(\bar{Y}_{01} - \bar{Y}_{00}\big)

where \bar{Y}_{tg} is the sample mean of Y in group g under treatment t.

K.4 Survival analysis

Definition K.4 (Survival function). For a non-negative random variable T, the survival function is S(t) = P(T > t).

Definition K.5 (Kaplan–Meier estimator). For distinct event times t_1 < t_2 < \cdots,

\hat{S}(t) = \prod_{j : t_j \leq t} \left(1 - \frac{d_j}{n_j}\right)

where d_j is the number of events at t_j and n_j is the number at risk.

Definition K.6 (Restricted mean survival time). For a budget C,

\mathrm{RMST} = \int_0^C S(t) \, dt.

K.5 The Buckley–James method

The Buckley–James (BJ) method (Buckley & James, 1979) is a semiparametric estimator for the accelerated failure time (AFT) model. It accommodates arbitrary censoring mechanisms and remains consistent under mild conditions. The BJ Boost Q-learning framework for counterfactual survival analysis directly models conditional survival time and avoids the restrictive proportional hazards assumption, enabling unbiased estimation of stage-specific Q-functions.

K.6 Concentration inequalities

Theorem K.1 (Hoeffding). For independent bounded random variables X_i \in [a_i, b_i],

P\left(\left|\sum_i X_i - \mathbb{E}\left[\sum_i X_i\right]\right| \geq t\right) \leq 2\exp\left(-\frac{2t^2}{\sum_i (b_i - a_i)^2}\right).

Theorem K.2 (Bernstein). For independent zero-mean random variables with |X_i| \leq M and variance \sigma_i^2,

P\left(\left|\sum_i X_i\right| \geq t\right) \leq 2\exp\left(-\frac{t^2/2}{\sum_i \sigma_i^2 + Mt/3}\right).

K.7 Le Cam's method

Theorem K.3 (Le Cam's two-point lemma). Let P_0 and P_1 be two probability distributions on a measurable space, and let \theta(P) be a parameter of interest. Then

\inf_{\hat{\theta}} \max_{j \in \{0, 1\}} \mathbb{E}_{P_j}[(\hat{\theta} - \theta(P_j))^2] \geq \frac{(\theta(P_1) - \theta(P_0))^2}{4} \left(1 - \mathrm{TV}(P_0, P_1)\right)

where \mathrm{TV}(P_0, P_1) = \frac{1}{2} \int |dP_0 - dP_1| is the total variation distance. This is the standard reduction for minimax lower bounds. The method relates minimax errors directly to a binary hypothesis test.

K.8 Bootstrap consistency

Definition K.7 (Bootstrap standard error). For a statistic \hat{\theta} estimated on n samples, the bootstrap standard error is the standard deviation of \hat{\theta} across B resamples drawn with replacement.

Theorem K.4 (Bootstrap consistency for DML). For double machine learning estimators, a bootstrap scheme where only the finite-dimensional parameter is recalculated in the bootstrap sample, with the nuisance estimand fixed from the original data, is theoretically sound because DML's orthogonality to the first-stage nuisance estimand ensures the source of the nuisance function is irrelevant. The centered bootstrap estimand converges to the correct limiting distribution in probability.

