Appendix L — Relation to Factorial Difference-in-Differences

The FDID framework of Xu, Zhao, and Ding (2026) provides the closest external validation of the H-FRAME identification strategy. Under canonical no anticipation and parallel trends assumptions, the DID estimator identifies only effect modification of the baseline factor G, not causal moderation. Identifying the latter requires an additional factorial parallel trends assumption, that is, mean independence between G and potential outcome trends. The H-FRAME compatibility contrast K_{ij} plays the role of the causal moderation estimand, and modularity (Assumption A.1) plays the role of the factorial parallel trends assumption.

The FDID paper formalizes four estimands: the effect modification \tau_{\mathrm{em}}, the causal moderation, and two others. Under canonical DID assumptions, the DID estimator identifies \tau_{\mathrm{em}} but not the causal moderation. The H-FRAME identification theorem is the analogue: modularity is the additional assumption that upgrades identification from effect modification to causal moderation.

---

Appendix M — Relation to Minimax Rates for Heterogeneous Causal Effects

Kallus, Mao, and Uehara (arXiv:2203.00837) derive minimax lower bounds for heterogeneous causal effect estimation using a localized version of the method of fuzzy hypotheses. The primary strategy is to construct distributions that are similar enough to be statistically indistinguishable but for which the parameter of interest is maximally separated. In the classic Le Cam two-point method, it suffices to consider a pair of distributions that differ locally; however, for nonlinear functional estimation, such pairs give bounds that are too loose. One instead needs to construct pairs of mixture distributions, which can be viewed via a prior over distributions in the model.

The H-FRAME minimax lower bound (Theorem E.2) is a two-point specialization of this general framework. The compatibility contrast K_{ij} is a linear functional of the interventional distributions, for which the two-point method suffices.

---

Appendix N — Relation to Interventional Boundary Discovery

Liu (arXiv:2603.18257) formalizes the discovery of the agent's Causal Sphere of Influence and proposes Interventional Boundary Discovery (IBD), which applies Pearl's do-operator to the agent's own actions and uses two-sample testing to produce an interpretable binary mask over observation dimensions. IBD requires no learned models and composes with any downstream RL algorithm as preprocessing. Across 12 continuous control settings with up to 100 distractor dimensions, IBD closely tracks oracle performance across all distractor levels. Critically, observational feature selection can actively select confounded distractors while discarding true causal dimensions, which motivates the interventional approach.

The H-FRAME MTCU extraction algorithm (Appendix G) extends IBD from observation dimensions to cognitive subgraphs. The consistency proof of Theorem G.1 relies on the consistency of IBD (Theorem F.1) and the faithfulness of the causal graph.

---

Appendix O — Relation to Buckley–James Survival Analysis

Lee and Kim (2026) propose a Buckley–James (BJ) Boost Q-learning framework for estimating optimal dynamic treatment regimes under right-censored survival data. By directly modeling conditional survival time, BJ Boost Q-learning avoids the restrictive proportional hazards assumption and enables unbiased estimation of stage-specific Q-functions. Grounded in potential outcomes, this framework ensures identifiability of the optimal treatment regime under standard causal assumptions. Compared to Cox-based Q-learning, which relies on hazard modeling and may suffer from bias under misspecification, the BJ approach provides robust and flexible estimation.

The H-FRAME survival analysis protocol (Chapter 8) uses the log-rank test as the primary analysis and the Buckley–James AFT model as the fallback when proportional hazards are violated. The BJ Boost Q-learning framework provides the theoretical justification for this fallback.

---

Appendix P — Relation to Modular Linkage Preservation

Qiao and Gallagher (GECCO '23) address the problem that crossover between neural networks is considered disruptive due to the strong functional dependency between connection weights. The modularity-based linkage model builds a proximity matrix by estimating dependencies between weights, then runs a community detection algorithm maximizing modularity to identify groups of parameters that are mutually independent and used as crossover masks. Results show that this approach finds better, more functionally dependent linkage which leads to more successful crossover and better performance.

The H-FRAME R1 operator (coupled recombination) is the analogue of modular linkage preservation: it preserves the parental associations between perceptual blocks and cognitive modules. The R2 operator (independent recombination) deliberately breaks these associations, testing whether exploratory novelty can outperform modular preservation. The R2 feasibility bound (Theorem C.1) formalizes the cost of breaking associations.

